# ZONE - Self-Hosted AI Stack Configuration
# ZERO CONFIGURATION REQUIRED! All variables have working defaults.
#
# Quick start (development):
#   cp .env.example .env
#   mkdir -p auth && htpasswd -cB auth/users.htpasswd admin
#   make up
#
# Production setup (with secure secrets):
#   ./scripts/setup.sh
#
# Variables are organized with prefixes for easy management:
#   DOMAIN_*         - Domain and hostname configuration
#   SECURITY_*       - Authentication and secrets
#   OLLAMA_*         - Model configuration
#   COMFYUI_*        - Image, video, and audio generation runtime
#   SEARCH_*         - Web search configuration
#   ZONE_MCP_*       - MCP servers for agent tools (magents, etc.)
#   VPN_*            - VPN and privacy settings
#   ZONE_VPN         - Full-tunnel overlay flag (synced with the vpn profile)
#   ZONE_VPN_REQUIRED - Override inferred public-web kill switch (empty = credentials)
#   COMPOSE_PROFILES - Optional Compose profiles (dev,vpn,monitoring,...)
#   DOCKER_VERSION_* - Docker image versions
#   DOCKER_DIGEST_*  - Immutable multi-platform image digests
#   ADVANCED_*       - Performance and system settings

# DOMAIN_* - Domain Configuration
# Shared base domain for Manager and other service subdomains.
# The variable name is retained for existing installations.
DOMAIN_HOST_WEBUI=webui.localhost

# Browser origins allowed to call the Manager API with credentials.
# Comma separated; entries may be full origins or bare hosts. A host also
# covers its subdomains, so zone.example.com admits manager.zone.example.com
# but never evil-zone.attacker.com. DOMAIN_HOST_WEBUI is always included, and
# localhost, 127.0.0.1 and ::1 are always allowed for development. Left empty,
# the API answers loopback only.
CORS_ORIGINS=

# APP_BASE_URL - The origin a browser uses to reach Zone, scheme://host[:port]
# with no path. Zone builds every outward link from it: the callbacks of the
# GitHub App that Connect GitHub registers and the page a browser lands on
# afterwards, the address GitHub delivers repository webhooks to, and the
# links in sign-up verification and password reset mail. Compose defaults it
# to http://manager.${DOMAIN_HOST_WEBUI}, and the dev profile to the Vite
# server, http://localhost:3001; outside compose it is http://localhost:3000.
# Set it when browsers reach Zone any other way, such as over https or on
# another host or port. A GitHub App keeps the origin it was registered with,
# so set this before connecting GitHub.
# APP_BASE_URL=https://manager.zone.example.com

# SECURITY_* - Security & Authentication
# CRITICAL: Change all keys before production deployment!
# Generate secure keys: openssl rand -base64 32
# Or use setup script: ./scripts/setup.sh

SECURITY_BASICAUTH_REALM="Zone AI Stack"
SECURITY_BASIC_AUTH_USERS_FILE=./auth/users.htpasswd
SECURITY_LITELLM_MASTER_KEY=
SECURITY_LITELLM_SALT_KEY=
SECURITY_SEARXNG_SECRET_KEY=
SECURITY_MANAGER_API_KEY=
SECURITY_LITELLM_UI_USERNAME=
SECURITY_LITELLM_UI_PASSWORD=
JWT_SECRET=
ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# PostgreSQL Database Credentials
# REQUIRED - No defaults! Generate secure values before starting.
# Quick start: ./scripts/setup.sh (auto-generates secure credentials)
# Manual: openssl rand -base64 32
POSTGRES_DB=litellm
POSTGRES_USER=litellm
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
# URL-encoded password for DATABASE_URL (set automatically by the setup script)
POSTGRES_PASSWORD_URLENCODED=

# HTTP to HTTPS redirect (false for local dev, true for production)
SECURITY_HTTP_REDIRECT=false

# Automatic TLS certificate generation via Let's Encrypt
# Requires: valid public domain, port 80/443 accessible, real email
SECURITY_GENERATE_CERTIFICATE=false

# OLLAMA_* - Model Configuration
# Browse models: https://ollama.com/library
#
# Recommended by hardware (setup.sh writes these from a chat preset):
#   8GB RAM:  chat only; fast=llama3.2:3b, reason=deepseek-r1:7b
#   16GB RAM: fast=llama3.1:8b, reason=deepseek-r1:14b, vision=llava:7b
#   32GB RAM: fast=llama3.1:8b, reason=deepseek-r1:32b, vision=llava:7b
# llava:7b needs 16 GB RAM. ./scripts/setup.sh --yes (all features) needs
# 64 GB RAM (Wan 2.2 A14B video) and ~174 GB free disk; it refuses all when
# that does not fit.

OLLAMA_MODEL_FAST=llama3.1:8b
OLLAMA_MODEL_REASON=deepseek-r1:32b
OLLAMA_MODEL_EMBED=qwen3-embedding:0.6b
OLLAMA_MODEL_VISION=llava:7b
# Engine location. Default is the host daemon so Docker Desktop / Apple
# Silicon can use Metal (measured ~113 tok/s vs ~0.6 in the container on
# an M4 Max). Install Ollama and keep it listening on 11434.
# Bundled container (Linux NVIDIA passthrough or CPU-only):
#   OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://ollama:11434
#   make up PROFILES=bundled-ollama
OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://host.docker.internal:11434
OLLAMA_HOST=0.0.0.0:11434
OLLAMA_KEEP_ALIVE=24h
OLLAMA_MAX_LOADED_MODELS=3
# Filesystem path used for the Models page disk-usage meter (default: /)
# ZONE_DISK_PATH=/
# Host RAM in bytes for Models → Features gates. setup.sh writes this so the
# manager container does not use Docker Desktop's VM size (vision needs 16 GB
# on the machine that runs Ollama/Comfy, not the container cgroup).
# ZONE_SETUP_RAM_BYTES=
# Where the manager computes embeddings for search/RAG.
#   ollama            - call OLLAMA_MODEL_EMBED on Ollama's native
#                       /api/embeddings at OLLAMA_BASE_URL (default)
#   local             - run OLLAMA_MODEL_EMBED in-process via ONNX Runtime
#                       (CPU); no network hop and no contention with the chat
#                       model. Weights are downloaded once into the
#                       manager_embed_cache volume.
#                       Needs a zone-server built with the `local-embeddings`
#                       feature, which is on by default.
#                       Runs the models the in-process engine knows
#                       (nomic-embed-text, mxbai-embed-large, snowflake, bge,
#                       all-minilm), not qwen3-embedding.
#   openai_compatible - call OLLAMA_MODEL_EMBED on the OpenAI-compatible
#                       LITELLM_HOST/v1/embeddings with LITELLM_KEY.
#                       Vectors wider than 1024 values are refused.
#                       Documents are split into chunks sized to the model's
#                       input limit: 3000 characters for qwen3-embedding and
#                       nomic-embed-text, 768 for a model Zone does not know.
EMBEDDING_ENGINE=ollama

# COMFYUI_* - Image, video, and audio generation
# Native Apple Silicon: keep ComfyUI on loopback. If Docker Desktop cannot
# reach it directly, use the authenticated bridge documented in docs/COMFYUI.md.
# Bundled NVIDIA: set COMFYUI_BASE_URL=http://comfyui:8188, run
# `make setup-comfyui-model` (and `make setup-comfyui-video-model` for Wan,
# `make setup-comfyui-audio-model` for ACE-Step, `make setup-comfyui-upscale-model`
# for upscaling), then `make up-comfyui`.
# COMFYUI_CHECKPOINT is the fallback image model when org/workspace AI settings
# do not set model_image. COMFYUI_VIDEO_UNET is the fallback video UNET when
# they do not set model_video, and COMFYUI_AUDIO_CHECKPOINT is the fallback
# audio checkpoint when they do not set model_audio.
# COMFYUI_UPSCALE_MODEL is the ESRGAN-family model chat upscaling runs, read
# from the ComfyUI models/upscale_models directory.
# COMFYUI_CLASSIFIER_MODEL is optional. Empty uses the chat/fast model that is
# actually installed. Org/workspace Fast overrides it when set.
COMFYUI_ENABLED=false
COMFYUI_BASE_URL=http://host.docker.internal:8188
# Required only when using scripts/comfyui-loopback-bridge.py.
COMFYUI_API_TOKEN=
# The header the token travels in, which the bridge checks too. Empty uses
# X-Zone-ComfyUI-Token.
COMFYUI_TOKEN_HEADER=
COMFYUI_WORKFLOW_PATH=/app/comfyui/workflows/sdxl-api.json
COMFYUI_CHECKPOINT=lustifySDXLNSFW_ggwpV7.safetensors
COMFYUI_VIDEO_WORKFLOW_PATH=/app/comfyui/workflows/wan2.2-a14b-t2v-api.json
COMFYUI_VIDEO_UNET=wan2.2_t2v_high_noise_14B_fp8_scaled.safetensors
COMFYUI_VIDEO_CLIP=umt5_xxl_fp8_e4m3fn_scaled.safetensors
COMFYUI_VIDEO_VAE=wan_2.1_vae.safetensors
COMFYUI_AUDIO_WORKFLOW_PATH=/app/comfyui/workflows/ace-step-v1-3.5b-api.json
COMFYUI_AUDIO_CHECKPOINT=ace_step_v1_3.5b.safetensors
COMFYUI_UPSCALE_WORKFLOW_PATH=/app/comfyui/workflows/upscale-image-api.json
COMFYUI_UPSCALE_MODEL=RealESRGAN_x4plus.safetensors
COMFYUI_CLASSIFIER_MODEL=
COMFYUI_CLASSIFIER_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=3
COMFYUI_CAPTION_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=60
COMFYUI_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=15
COMFYUI_GENERATION_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=300
COMFYUI_VIDEO_GENERATION_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=7200
COMFYUI_AUDIO_GENERATION_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=600
COMFYUI_UPSCALE_GENERATION_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=600
COMFYUI_POLL_INTERVAL_MILLISECONDS=500
COMFYUI_ARTIFACT_ROOT=/app/artifacts

# LoRA training from a submitted video.
# Frames are sampled above COMFYUI_TRAIN_FRAME_RATE so the sharpest frame of each
# moment wins its slot; repeats of a shot already kept are then dropped, which
# is why a clip can come back with fewer frames than its length times the rate.
# COMFYUI_TRAIN_FRAME_LIMIT caps what one clip contributes: the trainer's step
# ceiling means each extra frame past that is simply seen fewer times.
# ffmpeg and ffprobe ship in the manager image. Point these elsewhere only to
# use a build of your own; without them, images still train and videos are
# refused with a message saying why.
COMFYUI_FFMPEG=ffmpeg
COMFYUI_FFPROBE=ffprobe
COMFYUI_TRAIN_FRAME_RATE=4
COMFYUI_TRAIN_FRAME_LIMIT=48

# U2-Net weights that frame every training crop on its subject, so a dataset
# keeps what it is training on rather than whatever was in the middle of the
# shot. `make setup-vision-model` writes them into the shared models volume at
# vision/u2net.onnx (~168 MiB), which is where this looks when it is empty.
# Without them a photo is cropped on its centre and a video frame on whatever
# moved.
ZONE_VISION_MODEL=
# Megabytes one training request may carry. Dataset images are posted in
# batches; this bounds one batch or one clip.
TRAIN_UPLOAD_LIMIT_MB=4096

# SEARCH_* - Web Search Configuration
# Used by Zone (zone-server) chat.
# Queries go to SearXNG on the Gluetun network namespace (`gluetun:8080`).
# Note: Web search only works when the vpn profile is enabled
# Start with: make up-vpn  or  make up PROFILES=dev,vpn,monitoring

SEARCH_ENABLE_WEB_SEARCH=true
SEARCH_RESULT_COUNT=5
SEARCH_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=15
SEARCH_SEARXNG_QUERY_URL="http://gluetun:8080/search?q=<query>&format=json"
SEARCH_SEARXNG_SERVER_BASE_URL="http://localhost:8080"
SEARCH_SEARXNG_INSTANCE_NAME="Zone Search"

# GitHub REST origin. Override for GitHub Enterprise Server, e.g.
# https://github.example.com/api/v3 . Publication and pull-request sync both
# use it; unset means api.github.com.
GITHUB_API_URL=
# Accept a plain http:// GITHUB_API_URL or GITHUB_WEB_URL, such as a loopback
# or LAN host you run. The repository token then crosses the network
# unencrypted, so an http:// origin is refused unless this is true.
GITHUB_API_ALLOW_HTTP=false
# GitHub web origin where Connect GitHub registers and installs Zone's GitHub
# App. Unset means github.com for api.github.com, otherwise GITHUB_API_URL
# without its trailing /api/v3.
GITHUB_WEB_URL=

# Optional HTTP proxy for remote model catalog searches. `make up-vpn` sets
# this in .env to Gluetun's proxy; `make up` clears it for direct requests.
# VPN-attached Manager reaches that proxy at http://127.0.0.1:8888 (shared netns).
MODEL_SEARCH_PROXY_URL=

# Optional HTTP proxy for proxy-aware tool commands, MCP subprocesses, HTTP
# MCP servers, evaluation tool runs and `zone-runner` jobs. When set, it takes
# precedence over ABNEGATE_EXEC_PROXY_URL and ABNEGATE_EXEC_PROXY_BYPASS for
# all of them.
# Configured proxy failures do not fall back to direct requests.
# VPN-attached Manager uses http://127.0.0.1:8888 for the same reason.
TOOL_RUNNER_PROXY_URL=

# Written by `make up-vpn` (`1`) and cleared by `make up`. Kept in sync with
# the `vpn` Compose profile so internet-facing services share Gluetun's
# network namespace (full tunnel + kill switch).
ZONE_VPN=

# When 1, public web (search, page fetch, curl/wget) stays offline unless
# ZONE_VPN is on. When 0, public web is allowed even with VPN credentials.
# Empty infers from credentials: a WireGuard private key or OpenVPN user
# keeps public web offline while the tunnel is down. `make up-vpn` writes 1
# the first time if this is empty.
ZONE_VPN_REQUIRED=

# COMPOSE_PROFILES - Optional Compose profiles (comma-separated)
# Combine any of: dev, vpn, monitoring, bundled-ollama, bundled-comfyui
# Overlay files are selected automatically (`dev` → docker-compose.dev.yml,
# `vpn` → docker-compose.vpn.yml).
#   make up PROFILES=dev,vpn,monitoring
#   ./scripts/compose.sh --profile dev --profile vpn --profile monitoring up -d
# `make up` (no PROFILES) starts core services only and clears this value.
# scripts/compose.sh writes COMPOSE_FILE when overlays are needed and removes
# it for core-only launches (an empty value is invalid for Docker Compose).
COMPOSE_PROFILES=

# VPN_* - VPN Configuration (Optional)
# VPN is OPTIONAL - system works perfectly without it
# To enable: make up-vpn
# Direct Compose: ./scripts/compose.sh --profile vpn up -d
# Providers: https://github.com/qdm12/gluetun-wiki

VPN_SERVICE_PROVIDER=surfshark
VPN_TYPE=openvpn

# OpenVPN Configuration (use if VPN_TYPE=openvpn)
VPN_OPENVPN_USER=
VPN_OPENVPN_PASSWORD=

# WireGuard Configuration (use if VPN_TYPE=wireguard)
# Get these from your VPN provider's WireGuard configuration file
VPN_WIREGUARD_PRIVATE_KEY=
VPN_WIREGUARD_ADDRESSES=

# Optional: Server location (works for both OpenVPN and WireGuard)
# VPN_SERVER_COUNTRIES="United States"
# VPN_SERVER_CITIES=New York
# VPN_SERVER_REGIONS=California

# ZONE_MCP_* - MCP servers for agent tools (magents, etc.)
# Task execution and `zone run` attach MCP servers as extra tools: stdio
# servers, run as child processes, and streamable HTTP servers, reached at a
# URL.
# If magents is on PATH and no server is configured, it is registered
# automatically (`magents mcp`).
# Config JSON matches Cursor:
#   { "mcpServers": { "name": { "command", "args" } } }
#   { "mcpServers": { "name": { "url", "headers" } } }
# Children inherit the runner environment and per-server env overlay.
# When TOOL_RUNNER_PROXY_URL is set, its proxy policy takes precedence.
# Only configure executables you trust.
# Every enabled entry is started or contacted, URL ones included, and its
# tools reach every organization. Add "disabled": true to keep an entry off.
# A URL server's `url` and `headers` may hold ${VAR} or ${VAR:-default},
# which Zone fills from the server's environment. Wrap the JSON in single
# quotes here: Docker Compose fills ${VAR} in an unquoted or double-quoted
# value itself, with an empty string when the variable is unset. The variable
# must also reach the manager container: list it under `environment:` in
# docker-compose.override.yml. See docs/CONFIGURATION.md.
# ZONE_MCP_ENABLED=false turns off every MCP server, the ones organization
# admins add in Settings included.
ZONE_MCP_ENABLED=true
# ZONE_MCP_AUTO_CONNECT switches the `magents mcp` fallback. ZONE_MCP_AUTO_MAGENTS
# is its older name: the fallback is attached only while both are on, so
# either one set to false keeps it off.
ZONE_MCP_AUTO_CONNECT=true
ZONE_MCP_AUTO_MAGENTS=true
# ZONE_MCP_CONFIG=/path/to/mcp.json
# ZONE_MCP_SERVERS={"mcpServers":{"magents":{"command":"magents","args":["mcp"]}}}
# ZONE_MCP_SERVERS='{"mcpServers":{"docs":{"url":"https://mcp.example.com/mcp","headers":{"Authorization":"Bearer ${DOCS_MCP_TOKEN}"}}}}'
# Let organization admins add stdio (command) MCP servers in Settings. They
# start from the allowlisted environment plus their own `environment`, but run
# as the server's user, which can read any running child's environment from
# /proc. Turning this on gives every organization admin every other
# organization's stdio MCP secrets and, while an instance stdio server that
# inherits the environment runs (any `command` entry above, or the magents
# fallback), the instance's own keys. Single-tenant installs only. HTTP
# servers need no flag.
ZONE_MCP_COMMANDS=false

# ZONE_AUTO_* - Auto projects: a project that runs itself. Every agentic task
# is run unattended, its pull request is checked, reviewed by a model other
# than the one that wrote it (and by the review bots already on the repo),
# fixed until no finding is open, merged, and reported. All optional.
# ZONE_AUTO_ENABLED=true
# ZONE_AUTO_TICK_SECS=15
# ZONE_AUTO_PARALLEL_TASKS=3
# ZONE_AUTO_MAX_ACTIVE_RUNS=4
# ZONE_AUTO_MAX_RUNS_PER_TASK=3
# ZONE_AUTO_MAX_REVIEW_ROUNDS=4
# ZONE_AUTO_REVIEW_MODELS=
# ZONE_AUTO_REVIEW_REQUIRE_DISTINCT_MODEL=false
# ZONE_AUTO_REVIEW_BOTS=
# ZONE_AUTO_BOT_REVIEW_GRACE_SECS=600
# ZONE_AUTO_CHECKS_GRACE_SECS=120
# ZONE_AUTO_CHECKS_TIMEOUT_SECS=3600
# ZONE_AUTO_ADMIN_MERGE=true
# ZONE_AUTO_DELETE_BRANCH=true
# ZONE_AUTO_POST_MERGE_SECS=1800
# ZONE_AUTO_MAX_FIX_TASKS=5

# ZONE_GITHUB_* - The console's Pull Requests and Releases pages: how long
# what GitHub said about a source is shown before it is asked again, how many
# days of releases are listed, how many open pull requests are read per
# source, and whether a merge branch protection refuses is retried as an
# administrator. All optional.
# ZONE_GITHUB_PULL_REQUEST_TTL_SECS=45
# ZONE_GITHUB_RELEASE_TTL_SECS=120
# ZONE_GITHUB_RELEASE_WINDOW_DAYS=7
# ZONE_GITHUB_OPEN_PULL_REQUEST_LIMIT=50
# ZONE_GITHUB_ADMIN_MERGE=false

# ZONE_NOTIFY_* - Where the server's notices go: auto-project merges, pauses
# and completions, regression alerts and scheduled digests. Every notice also
# lands in the project's updates chat, so these are optional.
# ZONE_NOTIFY_SLACK_WEBHOOK=https://hooks.slack.com/services/...
# ZONE_NOTIFY_DISCORD_WEBHOOK=https://discord.com/api/webhooks/...
# ZONE_NOTIFY_EMAIL_TO=alerts@example.com,ops@example.com
# ZONE_NOTIFY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=10
# SMTP_* - The relay ZONE_NOTIFY_EMAIL_TO, sign-up verification and password
# resets send through. Port 465 connects with implicit TLS; any other port,
# 587 included, upgrades with STARTTLS before signing in. A blank value other
# than SMTP_PASSWORD counts as unset, and an unreadable SMTP_PORT falls back
# to 587. ALERT_SMTP_* below is Grafana's own.
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_USER=
# SMTP_PASSWORD=
# SMTP_FROM=noreply@example.com
# SMTP_FROM_NAME=Zone

# ZONE_LLM_BACKEND - The instance-wide default that serves completions:
#   litellm - the LiteLLM proxy (default)
#   claude  - the claude CLI in the manager image
#   codex   - the codex CLI in the manager image
# An organization or workspace that picks Claude Code or Codex in its AI
# settings runs that CLI whatever this says. Organization admins sign in to
# each CLI under Settings → Organization → AI defaults.
ZONE_LLM_BACKEND=litellm

# ZONE_AGENT_HOST_LOGIN - Whether an organization that has not signed in may
# use the server user's own claude or codex login instead. A native server
# defaults to true. Compose sets false: a login made inside the container
# would serve every organization.
ZONE_AGENT_HOST_LOGIN=false

# ZONE_CHAT_AGENT_CWD - Where chat tools start when the model gives a
# relative path. /app is root-owned in the manager image, so the default is
# zone's own /app/workspace. The dev profile defaults to /app/runner; a value
# here overrides both.
# ZONE_CHAT_AGENT_CWD=/app/workspace

# ZONE_HOST_ROOT - Host folder bind-mounted at /host inside the manager.
# Chat tools and filesystem sources see paths under it. Settings → Workspace →
# Folders (and the first-run setup step) pick directories under this root.
# Empty mounts an empty placeholder. Recreate the manager after changing it.
# ZONE_HOST_ROOT=/Users/you

# ZONE_CODEX_SANDBOX - How codex sandboxes its own shell when a chat grants it
# its builtin tools. codex sandboxes with bubblewrap, which needs user
# namespaces, and Docker's default seccomp profile blocks them. The image
# therefore defaults to danger-full-access, so codex's shell runs
# unsandboxed as the zone user. If your seccomp profile allows user
# namespaces, set workspace-write to sandbox it again. read-only is refused:
# a turn that withholds codex's tools already runs read-only.
# ZONE_CODEX_SANDBOX=workspace-write

# ZONE_ENDPOINT_HOSTS - The hosts organizations and workspaces may save
# endpoints on in AI Settings, comma-separated. A host matches only itself; a
# suffix such as .corp.example or *.corp.example matches that domain and every
# name under it. Empty allows every host. Link-local and cloud metadata
# addresses are refused either way.
# ZONE_ENDPOINT_HOSTS=api.openai.com,api.anthropic.com,.corp.example

# ZONE_AGENT_STATE_DIR - Where each organization's agent state lives. Every
# sign-in has its own claude or codex home, with codex's auth.json and the
# CLI's session files, under <org>/<agent>/logins/<id>, and an agent's sign-ins
# share the working directory <org>/<agent>/work. Claude tokens are sealed in
# the database instead. Compose and the Helm chart set it to /app/agent-state
# whatever .env says. A native server reads it from its own environment, and
# defaults to $XDG_STATE_HOME/zone/agents, or ~/.local/state/zone/agents. In
# compose it is the zone_manager_agent_state volume, which `make backup`
# archives, codex credentials included.
# ZONE_AGENT_STATE_DIR=/app/agent-state

# ZONE_PROJECT_CLONE_DIR - Where a project's GitHub repository is cloned when no
# folder of its workspace holds a checkout, for its chats to start in. Compose
# sets it to /tmp/repos, the zone_manager_repos volume, and the Helm chart to
# /app/clones, whatever .env says. A native server reads it from its own
# environment, and defaults to clones beside ZONE_AGENT_STATE_DIR.
# ZONE_PROJECT_CLONE_DIR=/tmp/repos

# ZONE_CLAUDE_TOKEN_URL - Where Claude sign-ins exchange and refresh tokens,
# for example through a proxy. It must be an http or https URL with a host
# and no credentials, query or fragment. Blank uses Claude's own endpoint.
# Zone reaches it through HTTPS_PROXY for an https URL or HTTP_PROXY for an
# http one (either in lower case too), falling back to ALL_PROXY, and directly
# when NO_PROXY lists its host. A proxy URL with a username or password fails
# the sign-in and every renewal with "Claude's sign-in cannot go through the
# server's proxy", without repeating the credentials.
# ZONE_CLAUDE_TOKEN_URL=https://platform.claude.com/v1/oauth/token

# ZONE_AGENT_USAGE_TTL_SECONDS - How long, in whole seconds, a sign-in's usage
# reading stays fresh. Zone reads each account's usage before it picks which
# one starts a chat or task, and reads it again once the reading is older
# than this. Anything but a whole number of seconds is refused at boot.
ZONE_AGENT_USAGE_TTL_SECONDS=60

# ZONE_CLAUDE_API_URL - Where Zone reads a Claude sign-in's usage and profile,
# for example through a proxy. Unset, it is Claude's own API.
# ZONE_CLAUDE_API_URL=https://api.anthropic.com

# ZONE_CODEX_API_URL - Where Zone reads a codex sign-in's usage, for example
# through a proxy. Unset, it is ChatGPT's own API.
# ZONE_CODEX_API_URL=https://chatgpt.com

# ZONE_AGENT_CALLBACK_PORT - The port of http://localhost:<port>/callback,
# where claude.com sends the admin's browser back with a Claude sign-in's
# code, so nothing is pasted, as claude's own sign-in does. Compose publishes
# it on 127.0.0.1 only and points ZONE_AGENT_CALLBACK at it. It works only in
# a browser on the machine Zone runs on, with the console open at a localhost
# address ZONE_CONSOLE_ORIGINS lists; any other console pastes the code
# claude.com shows instead, and the panel offers that either way, under
# "Paste a code instead". A browser on another machine can reach it through an
# SSH tunnel, ssh -L 54545:localhost:54545 -L 8080:localhost:80 <zone-host>,
# with the console opened at http://manager.localhost:8080 and that origin
# listed in ZONE_CONSOLE_ORIGINS. Set it empty to turn the callback off; the
# port is then left free.
# ZONE_AGENT_CALLBACK_PORT=54545

# ZONE_CONSOLE_ORIGINS - The consoles the callback sends a sign-in's browser
# on to with its one-time receipt, comma separated, each exactly as the
# browser names it: scheme://host[:port], with no path. A sign-in uses the
# callback only when it starts from one of them at a localhost address; from
# anywhere else, or with this empty, the code is pasted. Compose lists the
# consoles Traefik serves, http and https at manager.localhost and
# manager.${DOMAIN_HOST_WEBUI}, and the dev profile adds the Vite server,
# http://localhost:3001. A list here replaces those, so name every console you
# open, and only consoles you run; this one adds an SSH tunnel's to compose's.
# ZONE_CONSOLE_ORIGINS=http://manager.localhost,https://manager.localhost,http://manager.webui.localhost,https://manager.webui.localhost,http://manager.localhost:8080

# ZONE_CONNECT_URL - Origins a phone should type into the Zone app, comma
# separated, each scheme://host[:port] with no path. Compose cannot see the
# host LAN address, so set this to http://<lan-ip> (and a Tailscale IP if
# you use one). GET /api/connect returns these plus a reachable request host.
# ZONE_CONNECT_URL=http://192.168.1.10

# ZONE_AGENT_ENV_PASSTHROUGH - Extra variables chat and task tools may read.
# Comma separated names. Tools otherwise see only PATH, HOME, USER, SHELL,
# LANG, LC_*, TERM, TZ, TMPDIR, XDG_RUNTIME_DIR and the proxy variables, so
# the database URL, JWT secret and API keys never reach a shell or a
# transcript. Name only variables you are willing to see in tool output.
# The claude and codex CLIs start from abnegate-agent-cli's allowlist, which
# keeps only the 13 POSIX LC_* categories, and inherit the variables named
# here too, except what a CLI signs in with (CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN,
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN, OPENAI_API_KEY, CODEX_API_KEY,
# CODEX_ACCESS_TOKEN), claude's CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_REFRESH_TOKEN, the Bedrock,
# Vertex AI and Foundry sign-ins and ZONE_MCP_TOKEN, which a CLI never
# inherits from the server. ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL and OPENAI_BASE_URL named here
# are set on every claude and codex turn by Zone instead. Every value a turn's
# CLI inherits from this list is scrubbed from its failures and logs. In
# compose, a named variable must also be in the manager's environment list.
# ZONE_AGENT_ENV_PASSTHROUGH=CARGO_HOME,RUSTUP_HOME

# MONITORING_* - Prometheus & Grafana Configuration (Optional)
# Enable monitoring: make up PROFILES=monitoring
# Access Grafana at: http://grafana.${DOMAIN_HOST_WEBUI}
# Access Prometheus at: http://prometheus.${DOMAIN_HOST_WEBUI}

# Set to true if you want to use monitoring (reminder only - use --profile monitoring)
MONITORING_ENABLED=false
MONITORING_PROMETHEUS_URL=http://prometheus:9090
MONITORING_GRAFANA_URL=http://grafana:3000
MONITORING_GRAFANA_ADMIN_USER=admin
# Applied to the Grafana admin user on every container start (not only first boot).
MONITORING_GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
MONITORING_GRAFANA_TOKEN=
MONITORING_RETENTION_TIME=15d

# Alerting Configuration (Optional)
# Configure email alerts for critical system events
ALERT_ENABLED=false
ALERT_EMAIL_RECIPIENTS=admin@example.com
# Discord: Channel → Edit → Integrations → Webhooks → New Webhook.
# Phone push is Discord's own notification for that channel. No SMTP needed.
ALERT_DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL=

# SMTP Configuration for Grafana alerts
# Common providers:
#   Gmail: smtp.gmail.com:587 (requires app password)
#   AWS SES: email-smtp.<region>.amazonaws.com:587
#   SendGrid: smtp.sendgrid.net:587
#   Mailgun: smtp.mailgun.org:587
ALERT_SMTP_HOST=
ALERT_SMTP_PORT=587
ALERT_SMTP_USER=
ALERT_SMTP_PASSWORD=
ALERT_SMTP_FROM_ADDRESS=alerts@example.com
ALERT_SMTP_FROM_NAME="Zone Alerts"

